      <section id="overview" class="chapter">
        <div class="chapter-heading">
          <span class="chapter-number">01</span>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow dark-eyebrow">THE GLOBAL PICTURE</p>
            <h2>Economic output and carbon footprints vary widely</h2>
            <p>Begin with where emissions occur and how GDP per person relates to CO₂ per person.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-grid">
          <article class="panel map-panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>CO₂ emissions per person</h3>
                <p>Territorial fossil-fuel and industrial CO₂ · tonnes per person · <span id="map-year-label">2024</span></p>
              </div>
              <span class="chart-badge">Click a country to focus</span>
            </div>
            <div class="map-layout">
              <div id="world-map" class="world-map" role="img" aria-label="World map coloured by per-capita CO2 emissions">
                <div class="map-loading">Loading country boundaries…</div>
              </div>
              <div class="map-scale" aria-label="Map colour scale">
                <span>Lower</span>
                <div class="gradient-bar"></div>
                <span>Higher</span>
                <p>Gray = no matched data</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="map-insight">Hover or focus a country to inspect its data.</div>
          </article>
          <article class="panel scatter-panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>GDP and CO₂ across countries</h3>
                <p>One dot per country · colour indicates world region</p>
              </div>
              <span class="chart-badge" id="scatter-count">— observations</span>
            </div>
            <div id="global-scatter" class="chart chart-tall" role="img" aria-label="Scatter plot of GDP per capita against CO2 per capita"></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="scatter-insight"></div>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="story-strip">
          <strong>How to read this view</strong>
          <p>Countries in the upper-right have higher GDP and higher per-person territorial emissions. A cross-country association does not establish that GDP growth caused emissions to change.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="patterns" class="chapter">
        <div class="chapter-heading">
          <span class="chapter-number">02</span>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow dark-eyebrow">FROM SNAPSHOTS TO PATHWAYS</p>
            <h2>Countries follow different growth–emissions paths</h2>
            <p>Compare long-term changes from a common baseline. Every cell uses the same start year and selected endpoint.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <article class="panel">
          <div class="panel-heading panel-heading-wrap">
            <div>
              <h3>Country pathway matrix</h3>
              <p>Change in GDP and CO₂ per person from <span id="matrix-start-label">2005</span> to <span id="matrix-end-label">2024</span></p>
            </div>
            <div class="inline-controls">
              <label for="matrix-region">Region</label>
              <select id="matrix-region">
                <option value="All">All regions</option>
                <option value="Africa">Africa</option>
                <option value="Asia">Asia</option>
                <option value="Europe">Europe</option>
                <option value="North America">North America</option>
                <option value="Oceania">Oceania</option>
                <option value="South America">South America</option>
              </select>
              <label for="matrix-sort">Sort countries</label>
              <select id="matrix-sort">
                <option value="co2-desc">Largest CO₂ increase</option>
                <option value="co2-asc">Largest CO₂ decrease</option>
                <option value="gdp-desc">Largest GDP increase</option>
                <option value="name">Country A–Z</option>
              </select>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="matrix-legend">
            <span class="legend-key"><i class="swatch swatch-decoupling"></i>GDP ↑ · CO₂ ↓</span>
            <span class="legend-key"><i class="swatch swatch-growth"></i>GDP ↑ · CO₂ ↑</span>
            <span class="legend-key"><i class="swatch swatch-contraction"></i>GDP ↓ · CO₂ ↓</span>
            <span class="legend-key"><i class="swatch swatch-reverse"></i>GDP ↓ · CO₂ ↑</span>
            <span class="legend-explainer">Cell values, top to bottom: GDP % · CO₂ % · click a year to focus</span>
          </div>
          <div id="pathway-matrix" class="matrix-wrap">
            <div class="empty-state">Loading country pathways…</div>
          </div>
          <div class="matrix-reading" id="matrix-reading"></div>
        </article>
        <div class="overview-grid quadrant-grid">
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>Growth vs. emissions change</h3>
                <p>Percentage change over the selected analysis window</p>
              </div>
              <span class="chart-badge">Click a point to focus</span>
            </div>
            <div id="quadrant-chart" class="chart chart-tall" role="img" aria-label="Quadrant scatter plot of changes in GDP and CO2 per person"></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="quadrant-insight"></div>
          </article>
          <article class="panel interpretation-panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>Pathway guide</h3>
                <p>Classify direction first; investigate causes separately.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="pathway-key">
              <div class="pathway-item"><span class="pathway-dot decouple"></span><div><strong>GDP up · CO₂ down</strong><p>Per-capita absolute decoupling across these endpoints.</p></div></div>
              <div class="pathway-item"><span class="pathway-dot relative"></span><div><strong>GDP up · CO₂ up</strong><p>Growth with rising per-capita emissions; compare the rates.</p></div></div>
              <div class="pathway-item"><span class="pathway-dot contraction"></span><div><strong>GDP down · CO₂ down</strong><p>Both declined; this is not evidence of green growth.</p></div></div>
              <div class="pathway-item"><span class="pathway-dot reverse"></span><div><strong>GDP down · CO₂ up</strong><p>Emissions rose while output per person fell.</p></div></div>
            </div>
            <div class="caveat-box">“Decoupling” here refers to per-capita values between two endpoints. It does not show that total national emissions fell, nor explain why a change occurred.</div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="trajectories" class="chapter">
        <div class="chapter-heading">
          <span class="chapter-number">03</span>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow dark-eyebrow">COUNTRY CASE STUDIES</p>
            <h2>Compare development pathways on a shared scale</h2>
            <p>Rebase both measures to 100 at the start of the selected window, then compare a focus country with one peer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-grid">
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3 id="trajectory-title">GDP &amp; CO₂ index</h3>
                <p>Baseline = 100 · selected window <span id="trajectory-window-label">2005–2024</span></p>
              </div>
              <div class="heading-actions">
                <button id="export-country" class="button button-light" type="button">Export country CSV</button>
                <label class="select-label" for="trajectory-mode">Measure
                  <select id="trajectory-mode">
                    <option value="both">GDP and CO₂</option>
                    <option value="gdp">GDP per person</option>
                    <option value="co2">CO₂ per person</option>
                  </select>
                </label>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div id="trajectory-chart" class="chart chart-tall" role="img" aria-label="Indexed GDP and CO2 trajectories for selected countries"></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="trajectory-insight"></div>
          </article>
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>GDP-matched peers</h3>
                <p>Countries with GDP per person within 10% of the focus country</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="peer-summary" id="peer-summary"></div>
            <div class="table-wrap">
              <table class="data-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>GDP / person</th><th>CO₂ / person</th><th>CO₂ gap</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody id="peer-table"><tr><td colspan="4" class="table-empty">Select a focus country.</td></tr></tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-footnote">Peer matching is a descriptive rule, not a causal or like-for-like evaluation. Country size, industrial structure, trade and energy systems also differ.</div>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="story-strip">
          <strong>Index, not absolute values</strong>
          <p>An index of 120 means the indicator is 20% above its own baseline. GDP and CO₂ indices can share an axis because both are normalized; their absolute units are different.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="annual" class="chapter">
        <div class="chapter-heading">
          <span class="chapter-number">04</span>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow dark-eyebrow">SHORT-TERM TURNING POINTS</p>
            <h2>Annual growth can move differently from the long-run trend</h2>
            <p>Year-over-year (YoY) changes compare each observation with the previous year. The first year in the selected window has no in-window predecessor.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <article class="panel">
          <div class="panel-heading">
            <div>
              <h3>Annual GDP &amp; CO₂ change</h3>
              <p>Percentage change from the prior year · focus is solid, comparison is dashed</p>
            </div>
            <span class="formula-chip">YoY % = 100 × (this year ÷ last year − 1)</span>
          </div>
          <div id="annual-chart" class="chart chart-wide" role="img" aria-label="Year-over-year GDP and CO2 changes for selected countries"></div>
          <div class="panel-footnote" id="annual-insight"></div>
        </article>
        <div class="three-notes">
          <article><span>01</span><strong>The zero line matters</strong><p>Above zero means the per-person measure increased from the previous year; below zero means it decreased.</p></article>
          <article><span>02</span><strong>A falling growth rate can stay positive</strong><p>A change slowing from +8% to +3% is still an increase. It is not a decline until the YoY value turns negative.</p></article>
          <article><span>03</span><strong>Use both time scales</strong><p>Annual emissions can rebound while remaining below the long-term baseline. Compare this chart with the indexed trajectory.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="structure" class="chapter">
        <div class="chapter-heading">
          <span class="chapter-number">05</span>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow dark-eyebrow">STRUCTURAL EXPLANATIONS</p>
            <h2>Energy and economic structure offer clues to different pathways</h2>
            <p>Compare country-level energy use, energy mix and value-added shares, then inspect the three-factor Kaya accounting identity.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="data-limit-banner">
          <span class="limit-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
          <div>
            <strong>Coverage and definitions vary by source.</strong>
            <p>Energy supply, GDP and territorial CO₂ are joined by country code and year. Some energy-source shares and sector observations are missing; gaps are not imputed. The Kaya chart is an exact accounting identity for matched values, not a causal attribution, and the sources use different underlying definitions.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-grid structural-grid">
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>GDP composition: available country profiles</h3>
                <p>Value added as a share of GDP · World Bank · select a country</p>
              </div>
              <label class="select-label" for="structure-country">Country
                <select id="structure-country"></select>
              </label>
            </div>
            <div id="sector-chart" class="chart chart-tall" role="img" aria-label="Agriculture, industry, manufacturing and services shares for a selected country"></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="sector-insight"></div>
          </article>
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>CO₂ intensity vs. GDP</h3>
                <p>Tonnes of CO₂ per international dollar of GDP per person · selected year</p>
              </div>
              <span class="chart-badge" id="intensity-sample">— countries</span>
            </div>
            <div id="intensity-chart" class="chart chart-tall" role="img" aria-label="Distribution of GDP-normalized carbon intensity across baseline GDP groups"></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="intensity-insight"></div>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-grid structural-grid">
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>Energy intensity across baseline GDP groups</h3>
                <p>Total energy supply per person divided by GDP per person · selected year</p>
              </div>
              <span class="chart-badge" id="energy-intensity-sample">— countries</span>
            </div>
            <div id="energy-intensity-chart" class="chart chart-tall" role="img" aria-label="Distribution of energy per GDP across baseline GDP groups"></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="energy-intensity-insight"></div>
          </article>
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>Energy mix: <span id="energy-mix-country">selected country</span></h3>
                <p>Coal, oil, natural gas, nuclear and renewables as shares of total energy supply</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div id="energy-mix-chart" class="chart chart-tall" role="img" aria-label="Energy source shares over time for the selected country"></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="energy-mix-insight"></div>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-grid partial-kaya-grid">
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>Three-factor Kaya decomposition</h3>
                <p>Focus country · log change since <span id="kaya-start-label">2005</span></p>
              </div>
              <span class="chart-badge">GDP × energy intensity × carbon intensity</span>
            </div>
            <div id="kaya-chart" class="chart chart-medium" role="img" aria-label="Log-change contributions of GDP per person, energy intensity and carbon intensity of energy"></div>
            <div class="identity-formula"><code>CO₂/person = GDP/person × energy/GDP × CO₂/energy</code></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="kaya-insight"></div>
          </article>
          <article class="panel interpretation-panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div><h3>How to read the components</h3><p>Log changes add across the three factors.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="kaya-steps">
              <div><span>1</span><p><strong>Activity:</strong> GDP per person</p></div>
              <div><span>2</span><p><strong>Energy intensity:</strong> total energy supply per unit GDP</p></div>
              <div><span>3</span><p><strong>Carbon intensity:</strong> territorial CO₂ per unit energy supply</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="caveat-box">Energy comes from OWID's total-energy-supply dataset; CO₂ is territorial fossil-fuel and industrial emissions; GDP per person is PPP-adjusted. This source-mixed accounting decomposition is descriptive and should not be read as a causal attribution.</div>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="story-strip">
          <strong>Coverage note</strong>
          <p>World Bank region and income aggregates are excluded by matching ISO codes to the country-level GDP–CO₂ sample. Lower/higher GDP groups remain descriptive 2005 GDP deciles, not official development classifications.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="evidence" class="chapter">
        <div class="chapter-heading">
          <span class="chapter-number">06</span>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow dark-eyebrow">TEST THE GLOBAL PATTERN</p>
            <h2>How strong is the annual GDP–CO₂ relationship?</h2>
            <p>Each point shows a separate cross-country regression for one year and the selected region.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-grid">
          <article class="panel">
            <div class="panel-heading">
              <div>
                <h3>Annual linear-model R²</h3>
                <p>GDP per person as the sole predictor of CO₂ per person</p>
              </div>
              <span class="chart-badge" id="r2-current">Selected year: —</span>
            </div>
            <div id="r2-chart" class="chart chart-tall" role="img" aria-label="Annual R squared for cross-country GDP versus CO2 regressions"></div>
            <div class="panel-footnote" id="r2-insight"></div>
          </article>
          <article class="panel findings-panel">
            <div class="panel-heading"><div><h3>Read the evidence carefully</h3><p>What this analysis can and cannot tell us</p></div></div>
            <div class="finding-block">
              <span class="finding-label">DESCRIPTIVE RESULT</span>
              <p id="finding-summary">Loading the matched country-year dataset…</p>
            </div>
            <div class="finding-block">
              <span class="finding-label">INTERPRETATION GUARDRAILS</span>
              <ul>
                <li>R² describes the fit of a simple linear relationship in one cross-section; it is not a causal estimate.</li>
                <li>Country coverage, influential observations, inequality within countries and model form affect results.</li>
                <li>These are per-person territorial CO₂ measures; consumption-based emissions and land-use change are outside scope.</li>
                <li>Observed differences motivate further investigation; they do not prove a specific structural mechanism.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="methods-panel" aria-labelledby="methods-title">
        <details>
          <summary id="methods-title">Data, definitions &amp; methodology</summary>
          <div class="methods-content">
            <div>
              <h3>Sources</h3>
              <p>World Bank and Our World in Data GDP per capita (PPP), Our World in Data / Global Carbon Budget territorial CO₂ per capita, OWID energy supply and mix, and World Bank World Development Indicators sector value-added shares. Dataset notes and citations are in <code>data/SOURCES.md</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Country sample</h3>
              <p>GDP and CO₂ records are joined on ISO-3 code and year. Aggregate OWID rows are excluded from country-level models. Charts use only rows with both values; the displayed sample size can therefore vary by year.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Comparison groups</h3>
              <p>For a descriptive group contrast, “lower” and “higher baseline GDP” are the bottom and top deciles of countries with 2005 GDP data. This is a transparent income-level proxy—not a World Bank income classification or a definitive developed/developing label.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Derived measures</h3>
              <p>Index = 100 × value in year ÷ value in baseline year. YoY (%) = 100 × (value in year ÷ value in prior year − 1). GDP–CO₂ R² is computed from an ordinary least-squares line with intercept. CO₂ intensity = CO₂ per person ÷ GDP per person.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Limits</h3>
              <p>Territorial fossil/industrial CO₂ excludes land-use change and emissions embodied in imports. PPP GDP is in constant international dollars. Missing observations are not imputed. Correlation is not causation.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>
